<header><p class="enc-kicker">Work / monument · artifact</p><h1 class="enc-title">Tel Dan stele</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">9th century BCE</span> · <a href="/centuries/9th-century-bce">9th century BCE</a> · Also known as Tel Dan inscription</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tel-dan">Tel Dan</a> stele is a fragmentary <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aramaic">Aramaic</a> inscription of the 9th century BCE found in 1993–1994 at Tel Dan in northern Israel. Probably set up by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hazael">Hazael</a> of Aram-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/damascus">Damascus</a>, it boasts of victories over a king of Israel and a king of the '<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/house-of-david">House of David</a>', the earliest widely accepted reference outside the Bible to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/david">David</a>'s dynasty.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/tel-dan-stele.jpg" alt="Tel Dan stele" width="320" height="309" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Oren Rozen · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:JRSLM_300116_Tel_Dan_Stele_01.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Discovery and study</h2><p>The stele is a fragmentary stele with an Old Aramaic inscription in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/phoenician-alphabet">Phoenician script</a>. Fragment A was found in July 1993 by Gila Cook, a member of the team led by Avraham Biran, among the rubble of a wall at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tel-dan">Tel Dan</a>; fragments B1 and B2 followed in June 1994. They were found not in their original setting but reused, and Biran and his colleague Joseph Naveh published them in 1993 and 1995. The stele is now in the Israel Museum.</p><p>There is widespread agreement that the three fragments belong to one inscription and that B1 and B2 fit together, but less on how A fits with them. The earliest possible date is about 870 BCE, and Lawrence J. Mykytiuk thinks it could hardly be later than 750 BCE. A few scholars, Niels Peter Lemche, Thomas L. Thompson and F. H. Cryer, have proposed later dates, and Cryer and Lemche argued that it was a modern forgery, a view most scholars have rejected because the fragments came from controlled excavations.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Contents</h2><p>Most scholars identify the author, who is not named, as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hazael">Hazael</a> of Aram-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/damascus">Damascus</a>, an important regional power in the late ninth century BCE. In the reconstructed text the author tells how the king of Israel invaded his father's land, how the god <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hadad">Hadad</a> made him king and went before him, and how he defeated kings with their <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chariot">chariots</a> and horsemen. Biran and Naveh read the names of two victims as Joram, son of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ahab">Ahab</a>, king of Israel, and Ahaziah, son of Joram, of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/house-of-david">House of David</a>, though scholars are evenly divided on these identifications.</p><p>Biran's translation has the author kill seventy kings. André Lemaire argues that the reading rests on a very small part of a letter and that the text speaks of two kings and two thousand <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chariot">chariots</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reception and legacy</h2><p>The stele is the earliest widely accepted reference outside the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hebrew-bible">Hebrew Bible</a> to the name <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/david">David</a> as founder of a Judahite polity, and one of only four known inscriptions from about 1200 to 800 BCE that name Israel, the others being the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/merneptah-stele">Merneptah Stele</a>, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mesha-stele">Mesha Stele</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kurkh-monoliths">Kurkh Monoliths</a>. A minority of scholars dispute the reading bytdwd as &quot;<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/house-of-david">House of David</a>&quot;, since no word divider separates byt and dwd; they suggest a place name, a divine epithet or other words. The stele is generally accepted as genuine.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Tel Dan stele” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The earliest reference to the “House of David” outside the Bible appears on the <b>Tel Dan stele</b>, an Aramaic inscription found in northern Israel in 1993.</blockquote><a href="/empire/united-kingdom-of-israel">United Kingdom of Israel · At a glance</a></li><li><blockquote>The usurper Hazael then conquered much of Israel and destroyed Gath; the <b>Tel Dan stele</b> probably records his victories.</blockquote><a href="/state/aram-damascus">Aram-Damascus</a></li></ul></section>
